<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Niger</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of the Niger</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> is a landlocked country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, named for the Niger River, with over 80% of its land area in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. Parts of its territory lay within states such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem–Bornu Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>, the Sultanate of Agadez and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> colonised it during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scramble-for-africa">Scramble for Africa</a> as part of French West Africa, and it became a distinct colony in 1922. It became an autonomous state within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-community">French Community</a> in 1958 and gained independence in 1960. Since then Niger has experienced five coups d'état and four periods of military rule.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> is a landlocked unitary state bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> to the northeast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> to the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> to the south, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a> to the southwest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> to the west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> to the northwest. It covers almost 1.27 million square kilometres, which makes it the sixth-largest country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the second-largest landlocked one after Chad. Over 80 percent of the land lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, and the population of about 29 million lives mostly in clusters in the south and west. The capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niamey">Niamey</a>, stands in the southwest corner on the Niger River, from which the country takes its name. Some non-desert parts of the country suffer periodic drought and desertification.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">Stone tools</a> found at Adrar Bous, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilma">Bilma</a> and Djado in the northern Agadez Region date back as far as 280,000 years before the present. During the African humid period the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> was wetter and more fertile, a phase called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-sahara">Green Sahara</a> that favoured hunting and later agriculture and herding. By at least the 5th century BCE the territory was an area of trans-Saharan trade, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> groups from the north used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> as transport. Migration to the south, mixing of sub-Saharan and North African peoples and the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> went on for centuries, aided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-conquest-of-the-maghreb">Muslim conquest of the Maghreb</a> in the 7th century.</p><p>European explorers of the 19th century in the region included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mungo-park">Mungo Park</a> in 1805–1806 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-barth">Heinrich Barth</a> in 1850–55. For its first 14 years of independence <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> was ruled by a single-party civilian regime under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamani-diori">Hamani Diori</a>. Diori allowed French-led uranium mining at Arlit. He survived a planned coup in 1963 and an assassination attempt in 1965. Economic difficulties, droughts and accusations of corruption and mismanagement of food supplies led to the coup of 1974 that overthrew him. The seventh and most recent constitution, enacted in 2010, set up a multiparty semi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/presidential-democracy">presidential system</a>, and after a coup in July 2023 the country has been ruled by a military council led by Abdourahamane Tiani.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> are the largest ethnic group, at around 56 percent of the population, and the population is predominantly Muslim. Niger is divided into seven regions and one capital district, which are subdivided into 36 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>. Its economy rests on subsistence agriculture, some export farming in the south and the export of raw materials, including uranium ore. Niger belongs to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a>, the West African Monetary Union, the Niger Basin Authority and the Lake Chad Basin Commission.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Niger” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
